            <ArticleTitle>Metallo-Supramolecular Gels that are Photocleavable with Visible and Near-Infrared Irradiation.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>15857-15860</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1002/anie.201707321</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A photolabile ruthenium-based complex, [Ru(bpy)2 (4AMP)2 ](PF6 )2 , (4AMP=4-(aminomethyl)pyridine) is incorporated into polyurea organo- and hydrogels via the reactive amine moieties on the photocleavable 4AMP ligands. While showing long-term stability in the dark, cleavage of the pyridine-ruthenium bond upon irradiation with visible or near-infrared irradiation (in a two-photon process) leads to rapid de-gelation of the supramolecular gels, thus enabling spatiotemporal micropatterning by photomasking or pulsed NIR-laser irradiation.</AbstractText>
